data.description:
Pages loaded by `sleepy` never advance animation time. `requestAnimationFrame` callbacks never run, CSS transitions stay at their start value, and Web Animations (`element.animate`) stay at `currentTime` 0 in the `running` state. Timers (`setTimeout`) do run, so script-driven state changes work and only frame-driven work freezes.

Reproduced 2026-10-29 with the current `sleepy` build, compared against headless Chrome for Testing (a `Runtime.evaluate` over the DevTools protocol), which advances all three.

## Reproduce

Save this page and run `sleepy query file:///path/index.html --selector '#report' --wait-for 'body[data-done]'`:

```html
<!doctype html>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<style>
  .box { width: 120px; height: 40px; background: #2a52a8; }
  #trans { transition: transform 300ms linear; }
  #trans.moved { transform: translateX(200px); }
</style>
<div id="raf">rAF frames: 0</div>
<div id="trans" class="box"></div>
<div id="waapi" class="box"></div>
<pre id="report">pending</pre>
<script>
  let frames = 0;
  const tick = () => { frames++; requestAnimationFrame(tick); };
  requestAnimationFrame(tick);
  const trans = document.getElementById("trans");
  setTimeout(() => trans.classList.add("moved"), 50);
  const anim = document.getElementById("waapi").animate(
    [{ transform: "translateX(0)" }, { transform: "translateX(200px)" }],
    { duration: 300, fill: "forwards" });
  // After 1 s every animation should be finished, with many frames drawn.
  setTimeout(() => {
    document.getElementById("report").textContent = JSON.stringify({
      rafFrames: frames,
      transitionTransform: getComputedStyle(trans).transform,
      waapiPlayState: anim.playState,
      waapiCurrentTime: anim.currentTime,
    });
    document.body.dataset.done = "1";
  }, 1000);
</script>
```

## What happens

- **sleepy:** `{"rafFrames":0,"transitionTransform":"matrix(1, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0)","waapiPlayState":"running","waapiCurrentTime":0}`
- **Chrome:** `{"rafFrames":60,"transitionTransform":"matrix(1, 0, 0, 1, 200, 0)","waapiPlayState":"finished","waapiCurrentTime":300}`

## Likely cause (unverified)

The offscreen view probably never gets a display refresh (no display link or rendering update), so WebKit never runs the steps that fire rAF callbacks and advance the animation timeline. Timers don't depend on that, which is why `setTimeout` works.

## Why it matters

- Any rAF- or animation-driven UI looks dead in `sleepy`: a click that starts an animation leaves state unchanged, so `--session` flows misreport working pages as broken.
- End states of transitions can't be checked, and a page whose script waits on `animation.finished` or `transitionend` never moves on.
- The workaround today is to drive animations from `setTimeout` or to add test-only URL hooks that skip them, both of which change the page under test.

Related: `Ft2O4Q` (3D transforms render flat). Both push motion checks to Chrome.

The question, for Ben, once the spike lands: what does `shot` render with? The options visible now:
(a) the compositor path everywhere it can run (viewport, element, rect), with `takeSnapshot` only for what the compositor can't cover; the plan doc's stated preference if fidelity and reliability hold;
(b) the compositor path only when detection flags 3D;
(c) a flag (`--renderer compositor`) and detection's note pointing at it;
(d) park it in project/backlog.md with what would un-park it, keeping detection as the answer.
Also: does live hosting become the default host for one-shot verbs (which would fix ND4YmR but changes `--wait-for idle`), or stay per-operation?
Record the ruling in project/2026-11-06-true-3d-shots.md and detail the build leaf from it.
